Say who puts text in a control.

A field that draws nothing is either never given text or given it and losing it, and nothing
outside the control can tell those apart. CIDER_TRACE_CONTROLTEXT names a class substring, or star
for all, prints every setStringValue: and setAttributedStringValue: with the receiver and the
string, and stops after 40.

WHAT IT SETTLED IMMEDIATELY, and the answer was the opposite of what was expected. iTerm2 Settings
Advanced draws its values and none of its descriptions. With the trace watching every
iTermTableViewTextField in the run there are FIVE setter calls and every one is a value:

  iTermTableViewTextField setString len=2  "10"
  iTermTableViewTextField setString len=2  "10"
  iTermTableViewTextField setString len=9  "Helvetica"
  iTermTableViewTextField setString len=19 "0.20000000000000001"
  iTermTableViewTextField setString len=3  "0.5"

The description fields are never given text AT ALL, by any setter, and the trace firing five times
for that exact class is the control that makes the silence mean something.

SO THE PORT IS NOT LOSING THE TEXT. Both paths that could lose it are ruled out: NSControl
implements setAttributedStringValue: and forwards to the cell, and -[NSCell stringValue] derives
correctly from an attributed object value. Nothing is being dropped; nothing is being sent.

WHAT IS STILL UNEXPLAINED, stated as such. iTerm2 CAN measure those descriptions: with
usesDefaultHyphenation implemented in the previous patch, tableView:heightOfRow: returns real
heights computed from the description text, so the model has it. The population path for the
description column simply does not run. That is inside the application and the next step is a spy
on its own delegate method rather than another AppKit trace.

Gates: dts 69 of 69, patch record PASS, roster sweep 7 of 7 CONTENT and all seven unchanged against
the capture baseline, roster input 7 of 7.

